{
 "question": "Where does a command of the AC-3 family spend its time before it decodes anything, and does reading the syncframes of a stream with a cursor change anything but the time?",
 "method": "Every command of the family reads its syncframes through one loop of `crates/oadec-cli/src/eac3.rs`. It held four megabytes of the file in a buffer and removed each frame from the front of it, which moves what is left of the buffer to the front. The loop now moves a cursor, and gives up what it has taken when the buffer is filled again; the reads it makes and the thresholds it reads at are the ones it had. The time was measured from outside the process with `tools/bench.py --compare`, the binary of main and the binary of the change turn and turn about, five runs a command, on the two longest streams of each kind the corpus holds and two TrueHD streams as a control; then the binary of the change beside FFmpeg on the same streams (`--refs`). That nothing else changed was looked for four ways: the reports and deliveries of both binaries over the corpus (`tools/old_new.py --decode-all`); 320 copies of the fixture damaged the ways a reader of frames notices, put to both; the campaign of evidence 22 on main and on the change; and two tests of the reader that are about the stream and not about the buffer, which were also run against the reader of main. The streams of the corpus are media and are not in the repository; the damaged copies are made from the fixture by a seed.",
 "machine": {
  "processor": "Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700K",
  "logical_processors": 28,
  "system": "Windows 11"
 },
