 "what_the_reader_did_before_and_still_does": [
  "a run of bytes that is no frame and is longer than the buffer holds is reported in pieces, one a filling of the buffer: how many findings one run makes depends on where a read ends, the bytes it adds up to do not",
  "a frame is taken when a sync word follows it or the stream ends there, so the frame before a run of other bytes goes with the run",
  "the end of the stream, found by a read that gives nothing after the last frame was taken, is reported as a gap of no bytes, which the commands file nothing of"
 ],
 "reading": "A command of the AC-3 family that reads or decodes the channels takes 38 to 66 per cent of the time it took, and one that decodes the objects 84 to 89 per cent. The time went into moving what was left of a four-megabyte buffer to its front at every frame, not into decoding: the less a frame costs to decode, the more was gained. FFmpeg decodes the same streams 2.13 to 3.44 times faster than the change, where the baseline had it at 4.7 to 6.8 on E-AC-3; what is left is in the decoder. Nothing a command says or writes has changed: not over the corpus, not over 320 damaged copies of the fixture, not in the campaign."
}
